III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57bcbc75-d195-518f-bc0e-7dcf10346726" :question/math-academy-id "q-221924" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2∣(60x-45y)$\n- **II.** $9∣(60x-45y)$\n- **III.** $5∣(60x-45y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(0)-45(1)=-45,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have $60(1)-45(1)=15,$ which is not divisible by $9.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n60x-45y=5(12x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $12x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $5∣(60x-45y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a2b10a-72ed-5a68-86e5-a82fb8dc5d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453818} {:answer/id #uuid "90b33809-ffd1-57ab-985a-b8d17ec1bf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01a2b10a-72ed-5a68-86e5-a82fb8dc5d17" :answer/type {:db/id #